awesome, found the solution after digging in the widget examples. thanks again guys for pointing out a solution :+1: 

<pre>.on('autocomplete:selected', function(event, suggestion, dataset) {          
            var helper = search.helper; // search is an instance of instantsearch
            helper.addDisjunctiveFacetRefinement('categories', suggestion.name);
            helper.search();
          });</pre>

Here's what I came up with - I am sure there are better ways, but this works.
Note, it is presumed you do add relevant libs on your own, and I use this with Typesense, but it should also work with Algolia since it uses instantsearchJS

```
const customMenuSelect = instantsearch.connectors.connectMenu((renderOptions, isFirstRender) => {
            const { refine, items, widgetParams } = renderOptions;

            if (isFirstRender) {
                const select = document.createElement('select');
                widgetParams.container.appendChild(select);

                select.addEventListener('change', event => {
                    refine(event.target.value || '');
                });
            }

            const select = widgetParams.container.querySelector('select');
            select.innerHTML = '';

            // Add an 'All' option at the top of the select element
            const allOption = document.createElement('option');
            allOption.value = '';
            allOption.text = 'All';  // Adjust this label if needed
            allOption.selected = items.every(item => !item.isRefined);
            select.appendChild(allOption);

            // Add other options with counts
            items.forEach(item => {
                const option = document.createElement('option');
                option.value = item.value;
                option.text = `${item.label} (${item.count})`;  // Including count here
                option.selected = item.isRefined;
                select.appendChild(option);
            });

            // Re-initialize Select2 after updating options
            $(select).select2({
                placeholder: 'Select a Category',
                allowClear: true,
                width: '100%',
            }).on('select2:select select2:unselect', function (e) {
                refine(e.target.value || '');
            });
        });
```

And in the `addWidgets`:
```
...
customMenuSelect({
					container: document.querySelector('#categories-refinement-list'),
					attribute: 'Category'
				}),
...
```

This does produce a working select2 that is populated with values from the attribute, and updates accordingly when values are changed.
